# What Can Evolution Help Us Explain?

=== layout: two-panels

# *Why Evolution Matters*

@left

- How do disease-causing organisms change?
- Why can pests return after treatment?
- How do inherited differences become more common in a population?
- How are different species related?

@right

![Slide image 3](assets/lect11b_restored/image_003.webp)

=== layout: two-panels

# *Why Evolution Matters*

@left

- Related species inherit traits from shared ancestors.
- Inherited differences can become more or less common over generations.
- Different finch beaks work well for different foods.
- Trees help us compare which traits species share.

@right

![Slide image 4](assets/lect11b_restored/image_004.webp)

=== layout: section

# Gene Trees

=== layout: two-panels

# What Does a Gene Tree Show?

@left

![Slide image 5](assets/lect11b_restored/image_005.webp)

@right

- A gene tree estimates relationships among gene sequences.
- We compare sequences to estimate which share more recent common ancestors.
- A species tree follows species; a gene tree follows a gene.
- Their branching patterns can differ.

=== layout: two-panels

# Tree Terms

@left

- [*Tip*]{color=#003FFF underline=true}: an endpoint labeled with a sequence or species.
- [*Branch*]{color=#003FFF underline=true}: a line connecting an ancestor to its descendants.
- [*Node (branch point)*]{color=#003FFF underline=true}: a split representing a shared ancestor.
- [*Root*]{color=#003FFF underline=true}: the oldest ancestor shown. A rooted tree identifies this starting point.

@right

- [*Ancestor*]{color=#A719DB underline=true}: an earlier organism or gene copy from which later ones descend.
- [*Descendant*]{color=#A719DB underline=true}: an organism or gene copy that comes from an ancestor.
- [*Clade*]{color=#A719DB underline=true}: an ancestor and all its descendants.
- [*MRCA*]{color=#A719DB underline=true}: most recent common ancestor of the tips being compared.

=== layout: two-panels

# Interpreting Gene Trees

@left

![Original annotated branches, tips, and nodes](assets/lect11b_restored/image_006.webp)

@right

- Read from the tips toward the root to find shared ancestors.
- Branching order shows which tips share more recent common ancestors.
- Our examples label tips with sequences or species.
- Branch length shows time only when the figure gives a time scale.

