=== layout: section

# Reading a Gene Tree

=== layout: two-panels

# Understanding Branch Points

@left

![Original annotated branch points and evolutionary paths](assets/lect11b_restored/image_014.webp)

@right

- A branch point represents a shared ancestor.
- Follow its branches toward the tips to find its descendants.
- That ancestor and all its descendants form a [*clade*]{color=#003FFF underline=true}.

@citation

https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phylogenetic_tree

=== layout: two-panels

# Tracing Common Ancestors

@left

- [Trace two tips toward the root until their branches meet.]{color=#A719DB}
- [This is their most recent common ancestor (MRCA).]{color=#A719DB}
- [A and B share a more recent ancestor than B and C.]{color=#A719DB}
- [Tips drawn next to each other are not always closest relatives.]{color=#A719DB}

@right

![Original hand-annotated most recent common ancestor diagram](assets/lect11b_restored/image_015.webp)

@citation

https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Most_recent_common_ancestor

=== layout: one-panel

# Different Forms, Same Gene Tree

@body

![Original Khan Academy comparison of three equivalent tree forms](assets/lect11b_restored/image_016.webp)

- The same relationships among A, B, C, D, and E appear in all three drawings.
- Rotating a branch point changes tip positions, but keeps the same shared ancestors.

@citation

https://www.khanacademy.org/science/biology/her/tree-of-life/a/phylogenetic-trees

