=== layout: two-panels

# Polytomy in Gene Trees

@left

![Original annotated polytomy diagram](assets/lect11b_restored/image_019.webp)

@right

- [*Polytomy*]{color=#784B04 underline=true}: a branch point with three or more descendant branches.
- We may not have enough evidence to tell which split came first.
- This drawing does not identify a closest pair within that group.

@citation

https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polytomy

=== layout: two-panels

# Find the Distance Between Two Tips

@left

![Original scaled four-sequence tree](assets/lect11b_restored/scaled_tree_interpretation.svg)

@right

- [*Pairwise distance*]{underline=true}: the distance between two tips.
- Follow the path between them and add every branch length.
- These numbers label branches; they are not node ages.
- What are the distances a-c, a-d, and a-b?

=== layout: two-panels

# Add the Branches Along the Path

@left

![Original scaled four-sequence tree](assets/lect11b_restored/scaled_tree_interpretation.svg)

@right

- a to c: 3.2 + 3.2 = 6.4
- a to d: 3.2 + 3.1 + 6.3 = 12.6
- a to b: 3.2 + 3.1 + 2.4 + 8.7 = 17.4

=== layout: bookend

# Key Takeaways

@body

- Find shared ancestors by following branches from the tips toward the root.
- More recent shared ancestors indicate closer relationships.
- Rotating branches keeps the same relationships.
- A polytomy can leave the order of splits uncertain.
- Add branch lengths along a path to find the distance between two tips.

